## Why This Is Asked
Evaluates ability to translate privacy and safety requirements into a concrete, low-overhead component that precedes LLM calls and manages per-tenant policy enforcement.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant policy surface and versioning
- Lightweight, rule-based prompt classification
- Redaction of PII and disallowed content
- Caching decisions to minimize latency and repeated processing

## Code Example
```javascript
function classifyPrompt(prompt) {
  const p = prompt.toLowerCase();
  if (/ssn|credit card|password/.test(p)) return 'PII';
  if (p.includes('invoice') || p.includes('order')) return 'TRANSACTION';
  return 'GENERAL';
}
```

Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a dynamic privacy policy system that balances latency, data locality, and cross-tenant isolation in a real-world multi-tenant LLM gateway.

Key Concepts

- Adaptive policy routing between local and centralized privacy services
- Per-tenant privacy budgets and policy versioning
- Canary rollouts and drift detection
- Data locality, auditability, and fault tolerance

Code Example

```typescript
type TenantPolicy = { id: string; useCentralService: boolean; budget: number; version: number };
function selectRoute(tenant: TenantPolicy, latencyMs: number): 'local'|'central' {
  if (!tenant.useCentralService) return 'local';
  if (tenant.budget <= 0) return 'local';
  return latencyMs < 140 ? 'local' : 'central';
}
```

Follow-up Questions

- How would you monitor policy drift and automatically rollback canaries?
- How would you model privacy budgets and ensure fairness across tenants?

## Why This Is Asked
This measures practical ability to implement tenant-aware routing and observability in an LLM gateway, balancing simplicity with correctness.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant policy mapping
- Latency measurement and logging
- Safe defaults and fallback
- Cache invalidation on policy updates

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo routing logic
function routeRequest(tenantId, req) {
  const policy = getPolicy(tenantId) // {modelVersion, fallback}
  const version = policy?.modelVersion ?? 'v1';
  const backend = selectBackend(version);
  const t0 = performance.now();
  const res = backend.call(req);
  logRoute(tenantId, version, performance.now() - t0, res.success);
  return res;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test concurrent policy updates without impacting in-flight requests?
- How would you handle deprecation of an old model version without breaking tenants?
